WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:02.000
Hey, it's Pete.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.499
I'm here in Silicon Valley today and I'm talking
to Vida Ha, who's the co-founder of Ifeelgoods.

00:00:07.499 --> 00:00:08.499
Hey, Vida.

00:00:08.499 --> 00:00:09.499
VIDA: Hi

00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:10.499
PETE: How are you doing today?

00:00:10.499 --> 00:00:11.499
VIDA: Great.

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:12.499
PETE: Are you excited?

00:00:12.499 --> 00:00:13.499
VIDA: Yes

00:00:13.499 --> 00:00:14.499
PETE: You pumped?

00:00:14.499 --> 00:00:15.499
VIDA: Yes

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:16.499
PETE: Good.

00:00:16.499 --> 00:00:18.740
So in this episode of g33ktalk, we're going
to talk to Vita about her technology stack

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.820
and we have some really interesting things
we're going to talk about.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.439
Some infrastructure stuff, we're going to
talk about how to stop and prevent online

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:28.820
fraud, which is pretty cool.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.030
We're going to talk about some various data
related stuff that you guys are dabbling in

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.070
and using, which is cool.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.640
And yeah, we're just going to generally geek
out and have a good time.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.640
Are you ready?

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.640
VIDA: Sounds great

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:40.640
PETE: Alright.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.120
So, Vita, you were previously at Google, right?

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.120
VIDA: Yes

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.640
PETE: And now your role here is co-founder
of Ifeelgoods

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.640
VIDA: And Lead Engineer

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.640
PETE: Lead Engineer.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:49.640
Okay, cool.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.800
So tell us a little bit about Ifeelgoods,
what you guys do, just so we have some context

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.969
to as what your application is and what you
guys are doing.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:00:58.969
VIDA: Yes.

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:01.890
We are a digital goods incentive platform.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:07.940
That's big jargon that basically says we power
the redemption of free digital goods.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:13.550
Things like Facebook credits, Skype minutes,
E-gift cards, anything that's instantly redeemable,

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:19.040
that can be delivered electronically, so that
retailers can offer them to consumers in place

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.040
of discounts.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.220
PETE: Okay.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.150
So give me a typical use case, like how would
a customer or where would a customer see your

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.680
service in a purchase flow for e-commerce
site, for instance?

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.250
VIDA: Right, so they would see banners that
we would host on the retailers' side that

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.460
talks about the offers.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.420
So maybe in place of free shipping, you would
get $5 worth of free Facebook credits instead.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:47.270
And then at the end of the flow, after the
receipt confirmation page, you'd see a widget

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.930
that's hosted by us.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.860
We actually do the whole front end part of
it also.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.690
You'd Facebook connect, click it, we know
who your identity is and then we deposit credits

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:57.960
directly into your account.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:58.960
PETE: Okay.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.890
So this could be an incentive, alongside an
actual, it could be a hardware that's purchased,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.750
or some sort of incentive or digital promotion
that is just powered by you guys.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.240
VIDA: Exactly.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.860
You get to use the Facebook credits immediately
today even if it takes a week for your new

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.050
shoes or whatever to arrive.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.260
PETE: Okay, okay, cool.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.870
Tell me a little bit about your technology
stack.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.980
What are you guys using and what are you built
on?

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:32.690
VIDA: We are using Ruby on Rails as our web
development framework on top of Amazon, EC2

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.360
services all over the board.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.850
We also have to know Facebook APIs, all the
things like that.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.560
PETE: Okay.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.820
You mentioned before that you guys are using
a lot of different parts of the AWS stack,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:47.970
run me through those quickly.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.180
VIDA: There is, EC2, which is the servers
that we actually run.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.870
There's RDS which is our main SQL style database.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:06.970
We also use Simple DB, which is a more, a
more new flavor kind of SQL database.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.380
And then there's also S3 for storage.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:14.540
So we use that both for any kind of large
data that we want to save for offline processing

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:22.370
as well as just images, creatives, like things
that go on our website on our redemption flow.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.340
And then we use a couple of the other services.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:35.260
There is the load balancing layer that we
use automatically with Amazon, Elastic MapReduce.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.319
PETE: Cool.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:39.550
So you're all the way in the cloud.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:40.860
Do you have any servers at all?

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:41.860
VIDA: No.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:43.600
PETE: Do you have a backup server somewhere?

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.930
VIDA: No.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.540
You can back up things on Amazon.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:47.540
PETE: Alright.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.310
A lot of people use it for that, right?

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.580
S3 is cheap.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.720
VIDA: They triplicate everything, typically.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.130
So I'm not worried about it.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.300
PETE: Okay, Cool.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.970
Have you ever had an Amazon outage, just out
of curiosity?

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:00.970
VIDA: No.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:01.970
PETE: No?

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:02.970
Alright.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.140
VIDA: We've been lucky enough to not be hit
with that problem.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.140
PETE: Cool.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.960
Well if Amazon is listening, I'm sure they
would like to hear that.

